## 4. Headless CMS Options — Full Comparison

### The Core Question

Should you replace the custom JSON + GCS system with a proper headless CMS that provides a GUI?

### Option A: Strapi (Self-Hosted, Free)

> [!NOTE]
> Strapi is an open-source headless CMS with a content builder UI, REST/GraphQL APIs, and role-based access.

| Aspect | Details |
|--------|---------|
| **Cost** | Free (Community Edition). Enterprise starts at $29/user/mo |
| **Admin UI** | ✅ Full GUI — content types, media library, WYSIWYG, relations |
| **Self-hosted** | ✅ Run on Cloud Run or any VPS. You control the data |
| **Content modeling** | Visual content type builder — define fields, relations, components |
| **Media** | Built-in media library with upload providers (GCS, S3, etc.) |
| **API** | REST + GraphQL out of the box |
| **Auth** | Built-in user/role management with proper sessions |

**Pros:**
- Full GUI editor — no JSON knowledge needed
- Visual content modeling — can define all block schemas in UI
- Proper media management with thumbnails, alt text
- User roles (editor, admin, super admin)
- Webhooks to trigger rebuilds on publish
- Large plugin ecosystem
- Self-hosted = you own the data

**Cons:**
- 🔴 **Separate service to deploy and maintain** — another Docker container, another database (PostgreSQL/MySQL)
- 🔴 **Migration effort** — need to recreate all 28 JSON schemas as Strapi content types, migrate data
- 🟡 **Resource overhead** — Strapi needs ~512MB RAM minimum
- 🟡 **Learning curve** — team needs to learn Strapi admin + API patterns
- 🟡 **Strapi v5 is still maturing** — some plugins lag behind
- 🟡 **Node.js version constraints** — Strapi has specific Node.js requirements
- Content modeling must happen in Strapi, not in your codebase

---

### Option B: Payload CMS (Self-Hosted, Free)

> [!TIP]
> Payload is a TypeScript-first headless CMS that can run inside your existing Next.js app.

| Aspect | Details |
|--------|---------|
| **Cost** | Free and open source. No paid tiers |
| **Admin UI** | ✅ Full GUI — custom field types, rich text, media, relationships |
| **Self-hosted** | ✅ Runs **inside your Next.js app** — no separate service |
| **Content modeling** | Code-based config (TypeScript). Feels native to your stack |
| **Media** | Built-in upload with cloud storage adapters |
| **API** | REST + GraphQL + Local API (zero-overhead, same process) |
| **Auth** | Built-in with JWT, can use existing auth |

**Pros:**
- **Runs inside your Next.js app** — no extra service, no extra deployment
- TypeScript-first — content types defined in code, fully type-safe
- Local API — fetch content without HTTP overhead
- Beautiful admin UI out of the box
- Perfect for your stack (Next.js + TypeScript)
- Built-in media/upload management
- Supports MongoDB or PostgreSQL
- Custom React components in admin panel

**Cons:**
- 🔴 **Requires a database** — MongoDB or PostgreSQL (need to add to infrastructure)
- 🟡 **Migration effort** — need to define collection configs and migrate data
- 🟡 **Adds weight to your app** — more dependencies, larger bundle
- 🟡 **Newer project** — smaller community than Strapi (but growing fast)
- Admin UI is part of your build — changes affect your deploy

